Chapter 4
Inherent risk, residual risk, and what managing it is worth
المخاطر الكامنة والمخاطر المتبقية وقيمة إدارتها
Chapter 3 produced numbers. This chapter asks when those numbers are measured — before the company does anything about the risk, or after — and then asks the question that decides whether any of this work is worth doing: what does managing risk actually buy?
The two questions are connected. A risk measured before the controls is inherent risk; the same risk measured after them is residual risk; and the difference between the two, set against what the controls cost, is the value of risk management stated in money. Everything else claimed for it — stability, reputation, a lower cost of capital, better decisions — builds on that arithmetic.
Figure F804-01 is your map.
Figure F804-01. Chapter 4 roadmap: risk before controls, risk after them, and what the difference is worth.
