WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 3 put the roaster's expected loss at 267,500 a year and noted that every probability in a risk table is conditional on the controls that exist now. This chapter is about that sentence. Book 2's Chapter 2 priced risk into the cost of capital; section 4.4 collects what the saving is worth.
4.1  Inherent risk
Inherent risk is the risk in an activity before any management action — the exposure that exists because of what the business does, measured as though no control, no insurance and no procedure were in place.
It is a deliberately artificial figure, and that is the first thing to understand about it. Orontes's inherent food-safety risk is the risk of selling contaminated tahini with no testing, no supplier approval, no traceability and no hygiene regime — a situation that has never existed and never will. The figure is constructed, not observed.
Why compute something artificial? Three reasons, and the exam asks for them.
It shows what the controls are carrying. A residual risk of near zero can mean the activity is harmless or that an elaborate control structure is holding back something enormous, and only the inherent figure distinguishes the two. The difference is what would happen if the controls stopped working — which is exactly what happens in a crisis, a system migration or a year of heavy staff turnover.
It ranks activities for attention. Two processes with the same residual risk are not equally important; the one with the higher inherent risk deserves more oversight, more testing and more attention from internal audit, because it has further to fall.
And it prevents the circular comfort of assessing only what is left. A company that measures nothing but residual risk concludes that its risks are small because its controls work, which is a conclusion it has assumed rather than tested.
Two practical difficulties go with it, and a good answer mentions them.
Inherent risk is hard to measure, because nobody has observed the world without the controls. The estimate is a judgment anchored on industry events, on near-misses, and on what happened in the places where the control genuinely failed.
And "no controls" has to be defined. Does inherent mean no controls at all, or no controls beyond those required by law, or the controls a prudent company would have anyway? Practice varies; the exam's position is the strict one — before any management action — and an answer should say which basis it is using.
