4.3  Reading the two together
The pair of figures is more informative than either alone, and the exam rewards an answer that reads them together. Figure F804-02 shows four combinations and what each means.
Figure F804-02. What the pair of figures tells you
@table
Inherent | Residual | What it means | What to do
High | Low | Controls are carrying a great deal | Test them hard; plan for their failure
High | High | The risk is not being managed | Act: control, transfer, or stop
Low | Low | A genuinely small risk | Do not spend more on it
Low | High | Controls are making things worse, or the assessment is wrong | Re-examine the assessment
@end
The fourth row deserves a sentence, because candidates assume it is impossible. It is not: a control can create risk. A new system introduced to reduce error introduces implementation risk; an approval step introduces delay; a segregation of duties that nobody has staffed creates a process that stops. More often, though, the fourth row means the assessment is wrong — usually because the inherent figure was estimated as though the controls were partly in place.
Three further uses of the pair.
Prioritising assurance. Internal audit should spend its time where the gap between inherent and residual is largest, because that is where the company is most dependent on controls it has not recently tested.
Planning for failure. Chapter 7's continuity planning asks what happens when a control fails, and the inherent figure is the answer.
Reporting honestly. A board that is shown only residual risk sees a company with small risks. A board shown both sees a company whose small risks are small because of specific things that specific people do, which is a different and more actionable picture.
