4.5  Why the benefits are hard to prove
Risk management has a measurement problem that every candidate should be able to state, because exam questions on the benefits often turn into questions about this.
Its successes are invisible. The fire that did not happen leaves no trace, and a function whose entire output is the absence of events cannot point to anything. The losses it failed to prevent, by contrast, are extremely visible.
Its costs are immediate and certain, while its benefits are deferred and probabilistic — the same asymmetry that makes Book 3's long-lived projects hard to defend against a payback rule.
And the counterfactual is unobservable. Nobody can show what the loss would have been without the control, which is why the inherent-risk estimate of section 4.1 matters so much: it is the only available statement of the counterfactual, and it is a judgment.
Four ways of showing the value anyway, and an answer that offers them is answering the harder question.
Track near-misses and the events that controls caught. A control that stopped forty incorrect deliveries this year has a visible output, and the loss database of Chapter 1 is where it lives.
Report inherent and residual together, as section 4.3 argued, so that the gap is the stated output of the function.
Use the cost-benefit arithmetic of Chapter 9 on each significant control, so that decisions are documented in money at the time they are taken rather than defended afterwards.
And measure the second-order benefits where they can be observed: insurance premiums and terms, the credit rating, covenant headroom, the volatility of earnings over time, the number of surprises reported to the board.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: what risk management is worth at Orontes
Step 1. Loss reduction on the register. The five risks of Chapter 3 carry 264,747 of residual expected loss. The inherent figure for the same five is estimated at 940,000, so the controls remove 940,000 − 264,747 = 675,253 a year.
Step 2. What the controls cost. Maintenance, testing, approvals, the quality function and insurance premiums come to 410,000 a year.
Step 3. Net on the direct benefit alone. 675,253 − 410,000 = 265,253 a year, before any second-order effect.
Step 4. Add the cost of capital. Twenty basis points on 26,892,975 of capital is 53,786, giving 265,253 + 53,786 = 319,039.
Check. Against operating income of $4,176,000 that is nearly eight per cent, which is a defensible answer to a director who asks what the risk function is for. Two honest qualifications go with it. The inherent estimate of 940,000 is a judgment and the whole calculation rests on it. And the twenty basis points cannot be observed directly; it is inferred from what lenders and insurers say they price. State both, and the number is credible; omit them, and it is advocacy.
