LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Inherent" and "residual"
Both words are Latin and both are precise, which is unusual in risk vocabulary. Inherent means sticking in — a property of the thing itself, not of what anybody has done about it — and that is exactly right: inherent risk belongs to the activity. Residual means left over, and it carries the correct implication that something has been taken away. The pair survives translation well: Arabic الكامنة, latent or lying hidden, and المتبقية, remaining, carry the same two ideas, as do French inhérent and résiduel. The trap is elsewhere, in the word control, which in English accounting means a procedure that reduces risk, while in ordinary English it means power over something. A manager who says "we have that under control" is making the ordinary-language claim; a control in the technical sense is a specific, performable, testable act, and if nobody can name who performs it and when, it is not one.
