Chapter summary
Figure F804-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F804-04. Chapter 4 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Inherent risk is the risk in an activity before any management action — artificial, hard to estimate, and worth estimating because it shows what the controls are carrying, ranks activities for assurance, and prevents the circular comfort of measuring only what is left. Residual risk is what remains afterwards, and it is the figure to compare with appetite: the roaster's 620,000 − 267,500 = 352,500 of reduction for 95,000 of cost leaves 352,500 − 95,000 = 257,500 of net benefit, and the pattern of cheap early controls and expensive later ones means the question is always whether the next control pays. Read together, the pair tells you whether a small risk is genuinely small or merely well-controlled, where assurance should go, and what to plan for when a control fails. The benefits of managing risk run from fewer losses through stabler results, a lower cost of capital — twenty basis points on 26,892,975 of capital being 26,892,975 × 0.002 = 53,786 a year — the ability to take the risks the company is paid for, protection of reputation, better decisions, fewer surprises, governance compliance and cheaper insurance. And they are hard to prove because prevented events are invisible, costs are certain while benefits are not, and the counterfactual cannot be observed: the answers are near-miss data, reporting both figures, documenting the cost-benefit case at the time, and tracking the second-order measures that can be seen.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
