Case-style practice: the quality function
Orontes's new chief executive has asked why the company spends $410,000 a year on quality, testing, supplier approval and insurance when "we have not had a serious incident in six years."
Required. Answer the question, using the figures in this chapter. Explain what the six clean years do and do not prove, and say what you would put in front of the chief executive to make the case verifiable rather than merely asserted. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC4-1  Answer A. Before any management action.
B is wrong. That is residual risk.
C is wrong. Insurance is one of the responses.
D is wrong. That is a severity measure.
SC4-2  Answer A. It reveals the controls' load.
B is wrong. Auditors use it; they are not the reason.
C is wrong. Premiums follow the exposure as insured.
D is wrong. Profit is not its subject.
SC4-3  Answer A. The conclusion is assumed.
B is wrong. It understates them.
C is wrong. Frameworks ask for both.
D is wrong. A register is still needed.
SC4-4  Answer A. What remains after the responses.
B is wrong. That is inherent risk.
C is wrong. That is the worst case.
D is wrong. Zero is neither achievable nor desirable.
SC4-5  Answer A. 352,500 − 95,000 = 257,500.
B is wrong. That is the gross reduction.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. That is the cost.
SC4-6  Answer A. Appetite is the benchmark.
B is wrong. Inherent risk is the starting point, not the test.
C is wrong. History is evidence, not a standard.
D is wrong. A premium is a price.
SC4-7  Answer A. The controls are load-bearing.
B is wrong. It is important precisely because it is controlled.
C is wrong. The pattern is normal, not an error.
D is wrong. Transfer may not be needed at all.
SC4-8  Answer A. The largest dependence on controls.
B is wrong. Low residual risk may be well controlled or trivial.
C is wrong. Low inherent risk needs little attention.
D is wrong. Past losses are one input among several.
SC4-9  Answer A. Usually an error, occasionally genuine.
B is wrong. Good controls lower the residual figure.
C is wrong. Insurability is unrelated.
D is wrong. Appetite is a separate matter.
SC4-10  Answer A. Informed risk-taking is the forgotten benefit.
B is wrong. That is the one everybody names.
C is wrong. Premiums are a second-order benefit.
D is wrong. Compliance is routinely cited.
SC4-11  Answer A. 26,892,975 × 0.002 = 53,786.
B is wrong. That is ten times too large.
C is wrong. That is ten times too small.
D is wrong. That misreads the figures.
SC4-12  Answer A. Stability protects plans, covenants and capital costs.
B is wrong. Stability is not the same as higher profit.
C is wrong. Tax is unaffected.
D is wrong. Risk is reduced, not removed.
SC4-13  Answer A. Prevented events are invisible.
B is wrong. The costs are immediate.
C is wrong. The benefits are probabilistic.
D is wrong. Records exist; the counterfactual does not.
SC4-14  Answer A. 940,000 − 264,747 = 675,253.
B is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
C is wrong. That is the residual figure.
D is wrong. That is the cost of the controls.
SC4-15  Answer A. A caught incident is an observable output.
B is wrong. Inherent risk is unchanged by reporting.
C is wrong. Estimates are still needed.
D is wrong. The effect on capital costs is indirect.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Risk before any management action | Inherent risk.
Risk after the controls | Residual risk.
Reduction delivered by the regime | 352,500: 620,000 − 267,500 = 352,500.
Net benefit of that regime | 257,500: 352,500 − 95,000 = 257,500.
What residual risk is compared with | Risk appetite, and where it exceeds appetite something must change.
Why benefits are hard to prove | Prevented events leave no trace, costs are certain while benefits are not, and the counterfactual cannot be observed.
What it tells you | Two measurements and one subtraction produce the only defensible statement of what risk management is worth — and the subtraction depends entirely on an estimate that must be made honestly.
@end
