Case-style set: the quality function
What the six clean years prove. They prove that the controls and the luck together have been sufficient for six years. They do not prove that the risk is small, because what is being observed is residual risk — the risk after the $410,000 has been spent. Reading six clean years as evidence that the spending is unnecessary is the circular comfort of section 4.1: the conclusion assumes that the outcome would have been the same without the controls, which is precisely the thing nobody can observe.
What the arithmetic says. On the five risks in the register, inherent expected loss is estimated at 940,000 and residual at 264,747, so the controls remove 940,000 − 264,747 = 675,253 a year for 410,000, a net 675,253 − 410,000 = 265,253. Adding about twenty basis points of cost of capital on 26,892,975 of capital, worth 53,786, gives 265,253 + 53,786 = 319,039 — some eight per cent of operating income, from spending that produces no visible output at all.
What the figures depend on. The inherent estimate is a judgment. It should be stated as one, and it should be anchored on something: the industry's recall record, the two near-misses the goods-inwards check caught last year, and what happened at the mill that had no supplier approval process.
What I would put in front of the chief executive. Four things, so that the case is verifiable rather than asserted. First, the near-miss log: how many incorrect deliveries, mislabelled batches and out-of-specification results the controls caught this year, with what each would have cost. Second, the inherent and residual figures side by side, with the basis of the inherent estimate written down. Third, the insurer's position — what the premium and the deductible would be if the quality function were reduced, which is an outside party pricing the same question. Fourth, a proposal: rather than defending the whole $410,000, test the marginal items. Some of the spending will be the cheap, powerful early control of section 4.2 and some will be the expensive marginal one, and only a control-by-control cost-benefit analysis distinguishes them. That answers the chief executive's question properly, which is not "is quality worth anything?" but "is every part of this worth what it costs?"
